# GABRIEL -- one training tick every six minutes.
#
# Install this with scripts/install_namecheap.sh, which replaces only its own
# line and leaves the rest of your crontab alone.  If you prefer to paste it by
# hand: crontab -e, then add the line below (adjust the path if you unzipped
# somewhere other than /root).
#
#   */6  every sixth minute
#   *    every hour, every day, every month, every weekday

*/6 * * * * /root/astraarcengine-improved/astra/scripts/cron_evolve.sh

# Why six minutes, and why that is not a problem
# ----------------------------------------------
#
# Namecheap's shared-hosting acceptable-use rules (cPanel/Stellar) cap cron at
# one run per five minutes and a handful of concurrent jobs.  Those rules are
# about a machine shared with other tenants; they do not apply to a KVM VPS on
# a user-responsible plan, which is what this is written for.  The interval
# here stays above five minutes anyway, so the same line would be compliant if
# it were ever moved onto shared hosting -- where the rest of this would not
# run at all.  Check the current AUP for your own plan before assuming either
# way; Namecheap's terms are the authority, not this comment.
#
# The interval is not what bounds the work, and this is the important part:
#
#   * scripts/cron_evolve.sh takes /tmp/astra-evolve.lock with flock -n.  A
#     tick that finds the lock held logs one line and exits 0.  Ticks cannot
#     pile up, whatever interval you choose.
#   * one evolve round over the fit split at --budget 14 takes roughly 20-60
#     minutes on 4 vCPU.  So most six-minute ticks are no-ops by design, and
#     the schedule is really "start another round as soon as the last one
#     finished", expressed in cron.
#   * a shorter interval therefore costs a few milliseconds of shell per tick
#     and buys a faster restart after a round ends; a longer one just leaves
#     the box idle between rounds.  15 minutes is equally correct and slightly
#     tidier in the logs:
#
#       */15 * * * * /root/astraarcengine-improved/astra/scripts/cron_evolve.sh
#
# Everything the tick runs is nice 10 and capped at min(4, nproc) workers, so a
# web stack on the same VPS keeps its CPU.
